<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Shapes Around Us – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Shapes Around Us</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I See a Rectangle.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Recognize simple classroom instructions spoken slowly and clearly. Interactive: Respond to simple conversations on familiar topics by using gestures or by pointing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow simple classroom instructions about shapes using gestures or actions (e.g., "Point to the square." "Find the triangle."). Can point to and name shapes during activities when prompted (e.g., "What is this?" Student points to a circle and says, "circle"). Can respond to very simple shape-related commands by pointing.</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of new vocabulary and simple instructions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and displays large, colorful flashcards of shapes (square, rectangle, circle, triangle) on the board. The teacher points to each shape and says its name clearly and slowly. Then, the teacher leads a "Look Around" activity, saying "Look! I see a square table!" and pointing to a square table, or "I see a rectangle door!" to engage students with realia around the classroom. Students listen and follow the teacher's gaze.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard for a "square" and says, "Listen! Square. This is a square." The teacher repeats it slowly, emphasizing the /s/ sound. Then, the teacher holds up a "rectangle" flashcard, saying, "Listen! Rectangle. This is a rectangle," emphasizing the /r/ sound. The teacher models the instruction "Point to the square" by pointing to the square flashcard. The teacher then models "Find the circle" by walking to a circular object and touching it. The teacher uses exaggerated gestures and clear articulation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding by asking simple yes/no questions and using TPR.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a circle flashcard and asks, "Is this a square? Yes or No?" (Students respond by shaking head or saying "No").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher says, "Point to the triangle!" and waits for students to point to the correct flashcard. If a student points incorrectly, the teacher gently redirects by pointing to the correct one and repeating "Triangle."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Shapes Around Us."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, animated video (e.g., "Shapes Song for Kids") or a pre-recorded audio track featuring clear, slow narration describing objects with shapes (e.g., "I see a round clock. It is a circle." "I see a tall door. It is a rectangle."). Large shape flashcards (square, rectangle, circle, triangle, oval, diamond, star). Realia: a round ball, a square book, a rectangular box.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying shapes: Recognize and name shapes like squares and circles during classroom activities. Describing shapes: Use simple phrases to talk about shapes (e.g., "This is a rectangle.")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question (Visual Matching):</b> After playing the audio/video, the teacher asks, "What shapes did you hear?" and displays the shape flashcards. Students listen again and point to the shapes they heard mentioned. (e.g., "Did you hear 'circle'?" - students point to circle).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Point (True/False equivalent):</b> The teacher says a shape name from the audio (e.g., "circle") and points to a different shape (e.g., a square flashcard). Students listen and shake their head 'no' if it's wrong, or nod/point to the correct flashcard if it's right.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Command Following:</b> The teacher gives simple commands based on the shapes. "Listen! Find something square!" Students look around the classroom and point to a square object (e.g., a square tile, a book). "Listen! Show me a rectangle!" Students point to a rectangle flashcard or a rectangular object.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening, recognizing and identifying shapes and related instructions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Shape + noun (e.g., "a square table"), WH-questions ("What shape is it?"), Present simple ("That is a circle.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> square, rectangle, circle, triangle, oval, diamond, star, it, see, look, draw, find, be (is), round, square, tall, long, short, big, small, this, that.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> /s/ in "square", /r/ in "rectangle", initial sounds of shape names.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Listen and Point (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher places large shape cut-outs or flashcards on the floor. Students stand around them. The teacher calls out a shape name (e.g., "Circle!"). Students listen and quickly point to the correct shape. The teacher repeats this with all target shapes, varying the pace. Then, the teacher gives instructions like "Point to the big square" or "Point to the long rectangle."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. "What Shape Is It?" (Semi-controlled):</b> The teacher holds up a picture of an object (e.g., a window) and says, "Look! What shape is it?" Students listen to the question and respond by pointing to the corresponding shape flashcard (e.g., "rectangle"). The teacher can also describe: "It is round. What shape is it?" Students point to the circle. The teacher emphasizes the 'is' in "It is a circle."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. "Teacher Says" Shapes (Pair Interaction/Peer Check):</b> Students work in pairs with a set of mini shape flashcards. Student A (teacher role) says, "Teacher says: Point to the triangle!" Student B listens and points to the triangle. Student A checks if Student B pointed correctly. Then they swap roles. The teacher circulates, listening to student instructions and ensuring accurate pronunciation, especially /s/ and /r/.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-17 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Shapes Around Us," focusing on listening comprehension and appropriate non-verbal or minimal verbal response.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Shape Safari" - a listening-based scavenger hunt around the classroom.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives each student a simple checklist with icons of the target shapes (square, rectangle, circle, triangle).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives a clear instruction: "Find a rectangle!" Students listen and walk around the classroom, looking for a rectangular object (e.g., a book, a door, a desk). When they find one, they point to it and optionally say "Rectangle!" (or just point).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher continues with other shapes: "Find a circle!", "Find a square!", "Find a triangle!".</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">For an additional challenge, the teacher can add adjectives: "Find a big circle!", "Find a small square!".</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The activity emphasizes listening to the instructions and physically responding by finding and pointing to the correct shape in the real environment, connecting directly to the theme "I See a Rectangle." and scenario "Shapes Around Us".</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in understanding and responding to shape-related commands and questions, collaborative interaction during the shape hunt, and successful completion of the communicative task (finding and identifying shapes).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Shape Safari" and "Teacher Says" activities, noting individual student's ability to follow instructions and point to the correct shapes. The teacher makes a quick tally or checks off names for each shape recognized.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher holds up shape flashcards one by one, giving commands like: "Point to the circle!" or "Touch the rectangle!" Students demonstrate their listening comprehension by performing the correct action. The teacher can also ask, "What is this?" while pointing to a shape, expecting a gesture or a single-word response (e.g., "square").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible understanding of instructions (demonstrated through correct pointing/actions).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (pointing to correct shapes when heard).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of gesture/pointing in response to commands.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participation and attempt to engage in the collaborative activities.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are given a simple drawing sheet with faces (happy, neutral, sad).
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I hear 'circle' and point to it?" (Students circle happy face if yes, sad face if no).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I find a square object in the classroom?" (Students draw a checkmark or X next to a square icon).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher self-reflection for improvement and future planning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new shape did you hear today that you liked?" (Students can point to the shape on a poster).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or a little difficult to find the shapes?" (Students show thumbs up/down).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson to engage students in listening? (e.g., TPR, visual aids, repetitive commands, variety of activities).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments are needed for next time? (e.g., more direct modeling for specific shapes, slower pace for some instructions, more individual checking).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the pronunciation points (/s/, /r/) sufficiently highlighted and practiced? What scaffolds can be added? (e.g., more choral repetition, individual sound checks).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson introduced core shape vocabulary through listening and identifying. The next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension) will reinforce these same shape names by introducing them in a very simple, illustrated text, allowing students to recognize the written forms of the words they have learned to hear and identify.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>